# Contributing

Thanks for helping improve `{{packageName}}`.

## Icons come from upstream

The SVGs under `resources/assets/svg/files` are vendored, and a refresh
overwrites the directory wholesale. **Do not hand-edit a generated SVG** -- the
next sync silently reverts it. Fix the upstream source, or the sync recipe that
transforms it.

That holds even when an icon is malformed. The author of an icon set is not a
compiler, so a parse or render failure is fixed by hardening the read-time
pipeline in `ichava/core`, never by repairing the asset here.

## Code

- `declare(strict_types=1);` at the top of every PHP file, and type hints on
  every public method, parameters and return alike.
- `vendor/bin/pest` runs the suite.
- Conventional Commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `perf:`, `test:`,
  `chore:`.
- No `version` field in `composer.json` -- the version comes from the git tag.
  No lock file is committed; this is a library, and a lock records a resolution
  consumers never use.

## Releases and versioning

- **A version tag is pushed only for final code.** `vX.Y.Z` marks a release, nothing else. To try a
  release build first, push a pre-release tag, `vX.Y.Z-rc.N`, which uses up no release number. It runs
  the same release workflow, so the tagged commit needs its own `## [X.Y.Z-rc.N]` CHANGELOG section
  (the workflow refuses to publish without one), and it is published as a GitHub pre-release.
- **A public tag is never moved, deleted or reused.** Once a tag has been fetched, moving it makes
  consumers of the same version disagree. Composer and npm git dependencies resolve the tag afresh, so
  new installs get the moved commit while every existing lockfile and cache keeps the old one (an npm
  registry version cannot be republished at all). For a Go module, the module proxy keeps the copy it
  first served and `sum.golang.org` records its checksum, so `go` fetches after the move fail
  verification. The next fix gets the next number.
- **Give the repository a tag ruleset.** On a public repository, a ruleset blocking deletion,
  update and force-push of `v*` tags enforces this; GitHub's free plan gives a private
  repository no rulesets, so there the rule is held by practice.
- **A bad release is retracted, not replaced.** Name it in the CHANGELOG and the GitHub release notes, and leave it listed on Packagist, which has no per-version retraction (only a whole package can be marked abandoned). Then ship the fix as the next
  version.

## Reporting a vulnerability

Not here, and not in a public issue. See [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).
